- The guy's a crowd-pleaser.

256
00:10:36,033 --> 00:10:38,433
- You talk as if
he's still alive.

257
00:10:38,433 --> 00:10:39,400
- [Doctor] He is.

258
00:10:39,400 --> 00:10:40,667
- [Mike] He's only mostly dead.

259
00:10:40,667 --> 00:10:43,300
- [Doctor] I just picked
up a faint pulse beat.

260
00:10:43,300 --> 00:10:45,833
- Keep massaging the heart.
- I am, I am.

261
00:10:45,833 --> 00:10:47,367
- [Tom] Oh, that feels good.

262
00:10:47,367 --> 00:10:49,300
- An electric shock should
stimulate the motor area

263
00:10:49,300 --> 00:10:51,267
enough to enervate
the heart again.

264
00:10:51,267 --> 00:10:53,933
Then he won't need
any external stimuli.

265
00:10:53,933 --> 00:10:56,300
- Keep away from the motor area.

266
00:10:56,300 --> 00:10:58,367
You'll paralyze him for good.

267
00:10:58,367 --> 00:11:00,733
- Which would you rather
be, paralyzed or dead?

268
00:11:00,733 --> 00:11:02,067
- [You're trying to play God.

269
00:11:02,067 --> 00:11:03,667
- [Tom] Tag, You're God.

270
00:11:03,667 --> 00:11:05,533
- Not yours to make.

271
00:11:05,533 --> 00:11:07,200
- When the obstetrician has
to decide which to save,

272
00:11:07,200 --> 00:11:09,200
the mother or the child,
who plays God then?

273
00:11:09,200 --> 00:11:10,600
- [Mike] He's pregnant too?

274
00:11:10,600 --> 00:11:11,600
- It's part of the game.

275
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:13,200
- The game?

276
00:11:13,200 --> 00:11:16,167
The human body's not a jigsaw
puzzle to experiment on.

277
00:11:16,167 --> 00:11:17,533
- It's Gnip Gnop.

278
00:11:17,533 --> 00:11:19,100
- You're playing it safe
like the other doctors.

279
00:11:19,100 --> 00:11:20,700
- [Mike] Yeah, jagoff.

280
00:11:20,700 --> 00:11:23,267
- I'll save my breath.
Keep massaging the heart.

281
00:11:23,267 --> 00:11:25,033
You've already lost
your patient, doctor.

282
00:11:25,033 --> 00:11:26,233
I'm going to save mine.

283
00:11:26,233 --> 00:11:27,667
- [Tom] Ooh, that stung.

284
00:11:27,667 --> 00:11:29,267
- [Doctor] The pulse is coming
back stronger than ever.

285
00:11:29,267 --> 00:11:30,467
- Thank you, Grandma.

286
00:11:30,467 --> 00:11:32,000
- [Doctor] Unbelievable.

287
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:33,200
- Nothing is unbelievable

288
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:35,333
if you have the
nerve to experiment.

289
00:11:35,333 --> 00:11:38,367
I've been working on
something like this for weeks

290
00:11:38,367 --> 00:11:39,933
- In your laboratory?

291
00:11:39,933 --> 00:11:41,500
- [Mike] Yes, my laboratory.

292
00:11:41,500 --> 00:11:43,567
- I knew this would work if
only I had the opportunity.

293
00:11:43,567 --> 00:11:46,267
- We don't conduct
experiments on people.

294
00:11:46,267 --> 00:11:47,933
You should be sure
of the results.

295
00:11:47,933 --> 00:11:49,533
- Then they wouldn't
be experiments.

296
00:11:49,533 --> 00:11:51,167
- [Tom] Yeah.

297
00:11:51,167 --> 00:11:54,767
- I am now. Stop
massaging the heart.

298
00:11:54,767 --> 00:11:56,400
- [Tom] You were
enjoying it too much.

299
00:11:56,400 --> 00:11:58,267
- Let's see if it can
take over by itself.

300
00:12:00,267 --> 00:12:01,833
- Yep, I'll take it from here.

301
00:12:05,633 --> 00:12:09,733
- [Mike] Luke, join me or you'll
star in "Corvette Summer".

302
00:12:12,867 --> 00:12:15,633
- All right, close up the chest.

303
00:12:15,633 --> 00:12:17,133
- [Tom] Zip!

304
00:12:17,133 --> 00:12:19,367
- I'm about finished
with the cerebral area.

305
00:12:19,367 --> 00:12:21,467
- [Mike] Oh, leave
his area alone.

306
00:12:21,467 --> 00:12:22,667
- How's his pulse?

307
00:12:22,667 --> 00:12:24,133
- [Doctor] Strong and steady.

308
00:12:24,133 --> 00:12:26,000
- Oh, it's real strong
and steady, don't ya know.

309
00:12:28,667 --> 00:12:30,267
- [Tom] Geez, what
the hell is this?

310
00:12:30,267 --> 00:12:33,067
A long one, boy. Stringy too.

311
00:12:36,067 --> 00:12:38,100
- [Mike] There, put
your finger right there.

312
00:12:40,233 --> 00:12:42,933
- I'm giving him a little
piping. Think it looks nice.

313
00:12:44,733 --> 00:12:46,367
- Hey, you did
perform a miracle.

314
00:12:47,767 --> 00:12:49,933
I may not approve
of your method, but.

315
00:12:49,933 --> 00:12:51,300
- [Tom] The fans
seem to like it.

316
00:12:51,300 --> 00:12:52,733
- I am proud of your results.

317
00:12:58,233 --> 00:12:59,500
- [Mike] Anyway,
I can't find it.

318
00:12:59,500 --> 00:13:01,467
I think my class
ring's in, in him.

319
00:13:01,467 --> 00:13:03,667
- Don't worry.
We'll get it later.

320
00:13:03,667 --> 00:13:06,467
- [Tom] Geez, someone should
have invented the IV stand.

321
00:13:08,200 --> 00:13:11,133
- [Mike] Then take it
easy in the hallways.

322
00:13:11,133 --> 00:13:13,433
- [Tom] And so the little
fellow was wheeled into post-op

323
00:13:13,433 --> 00:13:14,633
for close observation.

324
00:13:16,300 --> 00:13:17,600
- I, I love you, Dad.

325
00:13:17,600 --> 00:13:18,833
- I love you, Son.

326
00:13:23,433 --> 00:13:25,800
- [Mike] Hey, you're not
my dad! You're Hitler!

327
00:13:27,133 --> 00:13:30,833
- An amazing, even an
extraordinary operation, son.

328
00:13:32,533 --> 00:13:35,100
Still, it's too risky,
too uncontrolled.

329
00:13:35,100 --> 00:13:36,700
- [Tom] Oh, don't
start, old man.

330
00:13:36,700 --> 00:13:37,633
- I saved his life.

331
00:13:39,133 --> 00:13:42,033
- And the aftereffects?
What about them?

332
00:13:42,033 --> 00:13:43,333
- What about them? They're fun!

333
00:13:43,333 --> 00:13:46,067
- You've lost the
urge to experiment.

334
00:13:46,067 --> 00:13:46,967
To explore.

335
00:13:48,367 --> 00:13:50,833
- [Mike] Order up. I need
those onion rings on the fly.

336
00:13:52,933 --> 00:13:53,900
- [Tom] You know,
they're doctors,

337
00:13:53,900 --> 00:13:55,633
but they do puppet shows too.

338
00:13:55,633 --> 00:13:56,667
- Oh, Judy!

339
00:13:56,667 --> 00:13:59,033
- Don't explore on people.

340
00:13:59,033 --> 00:14:01,133
Before you put a scalpel to one,

341
00:14:01,133 --> 00:14:03,533
an operation like this needs
testing under every condition.

342
00:14:03,533 --> 00:14:04,767
- Yeah. You want mayo?

343
00:14:04,767 --> 00:14:08,967
- Over and over again.
Rabbits, mice, monkeys.

344
00:14:08,967 --> 00:14:09,800
- [Mike] Humpty-back camels.

345
00:14:09,800 --> 00:14:11,100
- Not people.

346
00:14:11,100 --> 00:14:13,367
- That man who should be
dead now won't think so.

347
00:14:13,367 --> 00:14:15,067
- What do you say to that, huh?

348
00:14:15,067 --> 00:14:16,700
- There's more to
surgery than just being

349
00:14:16,700 --> 00:14:20,333
a carpenter to patch up walls
or a plumber or drain pipes.

350
00:14:20,333 --> 00:14:21,967
- Or a muffin baker
to bake muffins.

351
00:14:21,967 --> 00:14:23,567
- Our bodies are
capable of adjusting

352
00:14:23,567 --> 00:14:25,133
in ways we've hardly dreamt of.

353
00:14:25,133 --> 00:14:26,433
- [Mike] Can we not talk shop?

354
00:14:26,433 --> 00:14:28,167
- If we can only find the key.

355
00:14:29,333 --> 00:14:32,000
I'm so close now, so very close.

356
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,033
- You're different, son.

357
00:14:34,033 --> 00:14:35,433
- The key to what?

358
00:14:35,433 --> 00:14:37,033
- Complete transplantations.

359
00:14:38,133 --> 00:14:40,167
To be able to transplant
limbs and organs.

360
00:14:40,167 --> 00:14:41,767
- [Mike] To boldly
go where no man-

361
00:14:41,767 --> 00:14:43,833
- To be able to replace
diseased and damaged

362
00:14:43,833 --> 00:14:46,933
parts of the body as easily
as we replace eye corneas now.

363
00:14:46,933 --> 00:14:48,600
- [Tom] We'll call
em Snap-Tite Limbs.

364
00:14:48,600 --> 00:14:50,133
- So that the new parts
will join together

365
00:14:50,133 --> 00:14:51,333
as though they were born there.

366
00:14:51,333 --> 00:14:52,833
- [Tom] And rule the world!

367
00:14:52,833 --> 00:14:54,200
- It can't be done.

368
00:14:55,867 --> 00:14:56,933
- It can be done.

369
00:14:56,933 --> 00:14:57,767
- Can't!
- Can!

370
00:14:57,767 --> 00:14:59,100
- Can't!
- Can!

371
00:14:59,100 --> 00:15:00,133
- Can't can't can't can't!
- Can can can!

372
00:15:00,133 --> 00:15:02,633
(upbeat music)

373
00:15:33,233 --> 00:15:35,700
- With my new special compound
I've created, I'll do it.

374
00:15:35,700 --> 00:15:37,033
I know I can do it.

375
00:15:37,033 --> 00:15:38,000
- Sure, sure, that's
what you think.

376
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:39,500
- That's what I know.

377
00:15:39,500 --> 00:15:40,600
I know I'm close.

378
00:15:43,233 --> 00:15:44,533
- [Mike] What do you want?

379
00:15:45,667 --> 00:15:48,533
- I'm so proud of
you I could kiss you.

380
00:15:48,533 --> 00:15:49,800
- [Tom] And your name is?

381
00:15:49,800 --> 00:15:51,700
- Always promises.

382
00:15:51,700 --> 00:15:53,933
- Careful. Your father's
liable to report us.

383
00:15:54,800 --> 00:15:55,900
- Stop the floor show?

384
00:15:57,300 --> 00:15:58,800
When you to a married, it
won't be fun to watch anymore.

385
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:01,867
- Ooh, well, that's late night.

386
00:16:01,867 --> 00:16:03,100
- Your grandchildren
won't be test tube babies.

387
00:16:03,100 --> 00:16:04,267
- What does that mean?

388
00:16:04,267 --> 00:16:05,200
- You'd better
hurry if you want to

389
00:16:05,200 --> 00:16:06,600
catch that plane to Denver, Dad.

390
00:16:06,600 --> 00:16:09,033
You know that medical convention
can't start without you.

391
00:16:09,033 --> 00:16:10,167
- [Mike] You're the MC.

392
00:16:11,567 --> 00:16:13,800
- Jan, you'd better check
about my reservations.

393
00:16:13,800 --> 00:16:15,200
- Oh yes. I'll call the airport.

394
00:16:15,200 --> 00:16:16,133
I'll be right back.

395
00:16:17,367 --> 00:16:19,400
- [Tom] She's red hot, son.

396
00:16:19,400 --> 00:16:20,433
- Bill.

397
00:16:20,433 --> 00:16:21,700
- [Tom] Honey.

398
00:16:21,700 --> 00:16:23,600
- The line between
scientific genius

399
00:16:23,600 --> 00:16:26,167
and obsessive fanaticism
is a thin one.

400
00:16:26,167 --> 00:16:28,000
Now, I want you
on the right side.

401
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:29,267
- If I don't experiment,

402
00:16:29,267 --> 00:16:30,500
how can I hope to
perform operations

403
00:16:30,500 --> 00:16:32,200
like the one you
almost messed up.

404
00:16:32,200 --> 00:16:34,433
- But I can't cover
up for you any more.

405
00:16:34,433 --> 00:16:37,100
The superintendent
had it out with me.

406
00:16:37,100 --> 00:16:39,200
He thinks it's you who has
been stealing those limbs

407
00:16:39,200 --> 00:16:41,500
from the amputee operations.

408
00:16:41,500 --> 00:16:42,733
- So what if it is?

409
00:16:42,733 --> 00:16:44,267
- [Mike] That's
mildly unethical.

410
00:16:44,267 --> 00:16:45,833
I've got to have limbs for
my transplant experiments.

411
00:16:45,833 --> 00:16:47,267
- [Tom] They're delicious.

412
00:16:47,267 --> 00:16:48,700
- Well, you said test and
experiment, test and experiment.

413
00:16:48,700 --> 00:16:51,733
- Yes, but limbs and
organs taken from people?

414
00:16:51,733 --> 00:16:54,067
- I've got to have
them to work with.

415
00:16:54,067 --> 00:16:55,967
- Come on, give me the
keys to the morgue, Dad.

416
00:16:55,967 --> 00:16:57,800
- Sure, I've made
a few mistakes.

417
00:16:57,800 --> 00:16:59,000
- [Tom] Oops.

418
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:00,667
- But I've learned from
them. I've learned.

419
00:17:00,667 --> 00:17:02,300
- [Mike] So you've
learned, then.

420
00:17:04,767 --> 00:17:07,833
- Your reservations are
all set. 3:30 take-off.

421
00:17:07,833 --> 00:17:09,500
- [Tom] Anything else?

422
00:17:09,500 --> 00:17:12,867
- Well, what have you two
planned for the weekend?

423
00:17:12,867 --> 00:17:13,833
- Oh, nothing much.

424
00:17:15,233 --> 00:17:16,667
A quiet weekend.

425
00:17:16,667 --> 00:17:18,300
- You're sure you're not
going up to the country house?

426
00:17:18,300 --> 00:17:19,700
- We have a country house?

427
00:17:19,700 --> 00:17:21,567
- You're always
sneaking off up there.

428
00:17:21,567 --> 00:17:24,067
The place gives me the creeps.

429
00:17:24,067 --> 00:17:25,700
I should've sold it
when your mother died.

430
00:17:25,700 --> 00:17:26,800
- You can't sell that place.

431
00:17:26,800 --> 00:17:28,333
- [Mike] Wait, Mother's dead?

432
00:17:28,333 --> 00:17:29,500
- Well, I mean, it's nice
to get away from the city.

433
00:17:29,500 --> 00:17:31,233
I can work without
anyone snooping around.

434
00:17:31,233 --> 00:17:32,367
- You spend too
much time up there.

435
00:17:32,367 --> 00:17:33,200
- [Tom] Yeah.

436
00:17:34,467 --> 00:17:37,667
- All right, I'll see
you both in a few days.

437
00:17:37,667 --> 00:17:39,200
I have to clean up
and get out of here.

438
00:17:39,200 --> 00:17:40,133
- [Tom] And I don't want you

439
00:17:40,133 --> 00:17:42,267
reanimating anything, young man.

440
00:17:42,267 --> 00:17:44,500
- Fine operation or not, Bill,

441
00:17:44,500 --> 00:17:45,833
you're walking on thin ice.

442
00:17:46,667 --> 00:17:48,000
Don't go too far.

443
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,033
- And don't cry
over spilled milk.

444
00:17:50,033 --> 00:17:51,833
And don't count your
chicks before they hatch.

445
00:17:51,833 --> 00:17:53,000
I'm outta here.

446
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
- [Mike] Your
dad's such a boner.

447
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:58,700
- Oh, every time you touch
me, I go out of my mind.

448
00:17:58,700 --> 00:18:00,767
- [Tom] But please
wash your hands.

449
00:18:00,767 --> 00:18:02,300
- [Mike] Tissue, all right.

450
00:18:02,300 --> 00:18:05,267
- Bill, I want to get married.
I can't stand not having you.

451
00:18:05,267 --> 00:18:07,333
- You've been wonderful.

452
00:18:07,333 --> 00:18:08,633
- I'd rather be a bride.

453
00:18:09,767 --> 00:18:10,900
- In a few more weeks.

454
00:18:12,333 --> 00:18:13,700
Nothing will keep us apart.

455
00:18:15,100 --> 00:18:16,333
We'll be together.

456
00:18:16,333 --> 00:18:17,867
- Doodly doodly
doodly, we're apart!

457
00:18:19,400 --> 00:18:21,500
- [Mike] Oh, you taste
like Vince Edwards.

458
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:25,600
- Sylvia Plath, RN.

459
00:18:27,133 --> 00:18:28,533
- [Tom] (whistling) Taxi!

460
00:18:28,533 --> 00:18:29,733
- Dr. Cortner!

461
00:18:29,733 --> 00:18:32,267
- Could you pee in
the bell jar, please?

462
00:18:32,267 --> 00:18:33,100
- [Mike] Whoa.

463
00:18:34,967 --> 00:18:36,033
- I'm so glad you're here.

464
00:18:36,033 --> 00:18:37,867
I was afraid you'd gotten away.

465
00:18:37,867 --> 00:18:40,467
There was a very important
phone message that came for you.

466
00:18:40,467 --> 00:18:42,933
It sounded quite urgent.
I've been looking everywhere.

467
00:18:42,933 --> 00:18:45,033
It was from a man called Kurt.

468
00:18:45,033 --> 00:18:47,500
He called from
the country place,

469
00:18:47,500 --> 00:18:49,600
and he said something
terrible has happened.

470
00:18:49,600 --> 00:18:51,200
He wanted you to come right out.

471
00:18:52,200 --> 00:18:53,433
- Thank you.

472
00:18:53,433 --> 00:18:55,067
- [Mike] But I'm too
cool for emergencies.

473
00:18:57,067 --> 00:18:58,300
- You've always wanted to know

474
00:18:58,300 --> 00:19:00,300
what's kept me away from
you so many weekends.

475
00:19:01,467 --> 00:19:02,600
Have you got the
keys to your car?

476
00:19:03,767 --> 00:19:07,000
- The answer? Yes, she
has the keys to the car.

477
00:19:08,400 --> 00:19:11,167
- [Tom] The long, long trailer.

478
00:19:11,167 --> 00:19:14,000
- [Mike] Sid and
Nancy, American style.

479
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,700
- Why haven't you ever
taken me up here before?

480
00:19:16,700 --> 00:19:18,767
- Because the things I'm
working on don't need audience.

481
00:19:18,767 --> 00:19:19,967
- [Tom] It's performance art.

482
00:19:19,967 --> 00:19:22,500
- That telephone
call. What about it?

483
00:19:22,500 --> 00:19:25,267
- All right, all right,
hold off the questions.

484
00:19:25,267 --> 00:19:27,067
- Why the mystery, Bill?

485
00:19:27,067 --> 00:19:28,567
What's this all about?

486
00:19:29,733 --> 00:19:31,467
- We'll be there soon
enough. You'll see.

487
00:19:31,467 --> 00:19:32,900
- [Mike] Now shut up,
you're my trophy wife.

488
00:19:32,900 --> 00:19:34,500
- I've got to hurry.

489
00:19:34,500 --> 00:19:36,500
(suspenseful music)

490
00:19:36,500 --> 00:19:38,133
- Chukkas to the floor, hurry!

491
00:19:39,900 --> 00:19:42,167
- [Tom] Nick
Mancuso is Stingray.

492
00:19:44,500 --> 00:19:46,667
- [Mike] A sign lift
by ancient astronauts.

493
00:19:48,267 --> 00:19:50,867
(suspenseful music)

494
00:19:50,867 --> 00:19:52,700
- You will enjoy
the tight suspension

495
00:19:52,700 --> 00:19:53,800
of the Mercury Comet.

496
00:19:57,633 --> 00:19:59,633
- [Mike] Think I'll
have a Papa Burger. You?

497
00:20:01,367 --> 00:20:03,067
- Man, I want a pair
of those Chukkas.

498
00:20:04,400 --> 00:20:06,200
- [Tom] And can we stop
at Grandma Squeakers'?

499
00:20:06,200 --> 00:20:07,767
Can we stop at Turks' Inn?
- No.

500
00:20:07,767 --> 00:20:08,800
- [Tom] Can we stop
at the Big Steer?

501
00:20:08,800 --> 00:20:10,033
- [Mike] No.

502
00:20:10,033 --> 00:20:10,967
- [Tom] Can we stop
at Cranberry Cove?

503
00:20:10,967 --> 00:20:13,167
- Is he chasing James Dean?

504
00:20:13,167 --> 00:20:15,033
- [Mike] Wow. That
was my prom theme.

505
00:20:18,933 --> 00:20:20,900
- [Tom] What's this?

506
00:20:20,900 --> 00:20:22,833
Our fanatical physician
and his fickle fiance

507
00:20:22,833 --> 00:20:25,200
e speeding to their
country cottage?

508
00:20:26,100 --> 00:20:27,633
- [Mike] That's pretty good.

509
00:20:27,633 --> 00:20:31,033
- Mrs. Webb, you're doing very
well. Careful now, Mrs. Webb.

510
00:20:31,033 --> 00:20:32,900
You're over the center
line, Mrs. Webb.

511
00:20:34,400 --> 00:20:35,767
- [Mike] I've got to hurry.

512
00:20:37,500 --> 00:20:39,233
- [Tom] Stop sign,
what stop sign?

513
00:20:39,233 --> 00:20:40,400
Curve, what curve?

514
00:20:41,800 --> 00:20:43,900
- Ah, the road is attacking me!

515
00:20:46,133 --> 00:20:47,900
- [Mike] We now join
"Barefoot in the Park"

516
00:20:47,900 --> 00:20:49,233
already in progress.

517
00:20:50,433 --> 00:20:52,400
- [Tom] Look out,
look out, look out!

518
00:20:52,400 --> 00:20:55,000
♪ Leader of the pack ♪

519
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:58,067
- Oh man, I'm really tired
from the crash. Honey?

520
00:20:58,067 --> 00:21:01,833
Honey?

521
00:21:01,833 --> 00:21:03,400
- [Mike] Well, this
shouldn't be too,

522
00:21:03,400 --> 00:21:05,900
oh, honey, what are you doing?

523
00:21:06,933 --> 00:21:07,767
Ew.

524
00:21:11,767 --> 00:21:15,067
- [Tom] Diarrhea is like
a storm raging inside you.

525
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,100
- Shouldn't have eaten
at Jack-in-the-Box.

526
00:21:26,800 --> 00:21:28,200
- [Mike] Hi, I'm
William Proxmire.

527
00:21:28,200 --> 00:21:29,700
How are you doing?
Put her there.

528
00:21:32,933 --> 00:21:35,100
- [Tom] (sniffing) It
actually smells good.

529
00:21:36,567 --> 00:21:38,833
- [Mike] Honey
roasted. Honey roasted?

530
00:21:38,833 --> 00:21:41,267
(Tom laughing)

531
00:21:41,267 --> 00:21:43,833
- Here, put this on.
It'll keep you warm.

532
00:21:43,833 --> 00:21:45,533
- [Mike] Would you
give me that back?

533
00:21:45,533 --> 00:21:47,200
It makes you look tall.

534
00:21:49,200 --> 00:21:50,300
- [Tom] Is that what, oh!

535
00:21:50,300 --> 00:21:53,133
- Ew!
- That's her head!

536
00:21:53,133 --> 00:21:54,900
- [Tom] Ooh, yuck.

537
00:21:54,900 --> 00:21:57,967
Don't forget my purse! Honey!

538
00:21:57,967 --> 00:21:59,600
- The rest of it's
pretty crispy.

539
00:22:03,967 --> 00:22:06,367
- [Tom] You just had
to go fast, didn't you?

540
00:22:06,367 --> 00:22:08,933
Well look, Mr. Big Shot. Now
I don't have a body anymore.

541
00:22:08,933 --> 00:22:09,967
Are you happy?

542
00:22:12,367 --> 00:22:14,933
- [Mike] Guys, I remember
his fiancee as being taller.

543
00:22:14,933 --> 00:22:15,767
- Yeah.
- Yep.

544
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:18,933
- Thank you, God.

545
00:22:18,933 --> 00:22:20,733
Thank you so bloody much.

546
00:22:23,600 --> 00:22:26,300
- [Tom] I just realized what
I did. It's really gross.

547
00:22:35,233 --> 00:22:37,033
- [Mike] Briggens is at
the 20, he's at the 10.

548
00:22:37,033 --> 00:22:38,500
No one will catch him.

549
00:22:38,500 --> 00:22:40,033
- It's either gonna
win the Nobel Prize

550
00:22:40,033 --> 00:22:41,167
or the Heisman Trophy.

551
00:22:42,567 --> 00:22:43,900
- [Tom] What if people
get the wrong idea?

552
00:22:43,900 --> 00:22:46,200
This could really
be taken wrong.

553
00:22:46,200 --> 00:22:47,800
Gotta get outta here.

554
00:22:47,800 --> 00:22:49,500
- [Mike] Hi doctor. How
are you doing there?

555
00:22:49,500 --> 00:22:50,767
- Oh, no problem. No head.

556
00:23:07,967 --> 00:23:10,100
- Oh, hey Cambot, bring
it down here, okay?

557
00:23:10,100 --> 00:23:10,933
Come on, buddy.

558
00:23:12,333 --> 00:23:13,633
- Just yank it out. Don't
worry about the wires.

559
00:23:13,633 --> 00:23:15,067
- He doesn't know
what he's doing.

560
00:23:15,067 --> 00:23:16,300
You don't know what
you're doing, do ya, Mike?

561
00:23:16,300 --> 00:23:18,400
- You guys, I've been
studying the manuals

562
00:23:18,400 --> 00:23:20,133
ever since I got here.

563
00:23:20,133 --> 00:23:21,600
I figure if we can gain control

564
00:23:21,600 --> 00:23:22,833
of the inertial guidance system,

565
00:23:22,833 --> 00:23:25,300
we got a chance of
getting out of here.

566
00:23:25,300 --> 00:23:26,200
Anybody know what this does?

567
00:23:26,200 --> 00:23:27,233
- Uh, nope.

568
00:23:27,233 --> 00:23:28,500
- No, no way.

569
00:23:28,500 --> 00:23:29,867
I don't think it's
important anyway.

570
00:23:29,867 --> 00:23:31,100
- Just get rid of it then.

571
00:23:31,100 --> 00:23:32,567
- Just cut the big
red cable. Try that.

572
00:23:32,567 --> 00:23:34,433
- So why do you want to leave
so bad? You just got here.

573
00:23:34,433 --> 00:23:36,800
- [Mike] You've been watching
the same movie I have, right?

574
00:23:36,800 --> 00:23:38,367
A man's gotta try, fellas.

575
00:23:38,367 --> 00:23:41,900
Hey, guys. It's
a mess down here.

576
00:23:41,900 --> 00:23:44,767
There's old sandwiches,
Tom, you got like one, two,

577
00:23:44,767 --> 00:23:47,567
no, there's like four heads
down here, did you know that?

578
00:23:47,567 --> 00:23:48,767
- Sure.

579
00:23:48,767 --> 00:23:50,800
- There's a dozen copies
of "The Picardian".

580
00:23:50,800 --> 00:23:53,300
- Hey, those are mine. Those
are gonna be valuable one day.

581
00:23:53,300 --> 00:23:55,000
- The big red cable,
Mike, just cut it.

582
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:57,133
- [Mike] Which one?
There's about 20.

583
00:23:57,133 --> 00:23:59,033
- Um, the green one.

584
00:23:59,033 --> 00:23:59,900
- Okay.

585
00:24:03,300 --> 00:24:04,300
- Gee, boy.

586
00:24:04,300 --> 00:24:05,933
Oh boy. Gee.

587
00:24:05,933 --> 00:24:08,967
That's my fault. That was
the cheese compressor line.

588
00:24:08,967 --> 00:24:10,467
Sorry.

589
00:24:10,467 --> 00:24:11,967
- You guys,

590
00:24:11,967 --> 00:24:13,867
I don't think we're gonna
gain control from here.

591
00:24:13,867 --> 00:24:14,867
- Of course we're not.

592
00:24:14,867 --> 00:24:15,900
- Nah, heck no.

593
00:24:15,900 --> 00:24:17,367
We tried that a year ago.

594
00:24:17,367 --> 00:24:20,533
- Well, why didn't you tell
me? Geez, wasted all this time.

595
00:24:20,533 --> 00:24:21,467
- No, Mike, 'cause.

596
00:24:21,467 --> 00:24:23,000
- 'Cause if you find a way out,

597
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:24,933
then you'll go and you'll
leave us all alone up here

598
00:24:24,933 --> 00:24:26,133
and well, you'll be gone.

599
00:24:26,133 --> 00:24:27,600
- Yeah.

600
00:24:27,600 --> 00:24:28,800
- Guys, I'm trying to
figure out a way to get us

601
00:24:28,800 --> 00:24:30,467
all out of here, okay?

602
00:24:30,467 --> 00:24:33,500
- So, so like if you find a
big box marked "Hamdingers",

603
00:24:33,500 --> 00:24:35,033
you're not gonna?

604
00:24:35,033 --> 00:24:36,267
- Guys, there are no more
boxes marked "Hamdingers".

605
00:24:36,267 --> 00:24:37,467
I know that.

606
00:24:37,467 --> 00:24:39,800
Look, I promise I
won't leave you, okay?

607
00:24:39,800 --> 00:24:42,067
Come on, cheer up. Cheese fight!

608
00:24:42,067 --> 00:24:45,033
(Tom and Crow laughing)

609
00:24:45,033 --> 00:24:47,500
- Oh hey, cut it out!
- It's fun!

610
00:24:47,500 --> 00:24:48,800
- [Gypsy] Guys, guys!

611
00:24:48,800 --> 00:24:50,067
- What is it, Gypsy?

612
00:24:50,067 --> 00:24:51,967
- [Gypsy] It's not cheese.

613
00:24:51,967 --> 00:24:54,567
(all groaning)

614
00:24:54,567 --> 00:24:57,167
(upbeat music)

615
00:25:26,300 --> 00:25:27,500
- [Mike] If it's not
cheese, what is it?

616
00:25:27,500 --> 00:25:28,800
- [Tom] It tasted like cheese.

617
00:25:28,800 --> 00:25:29,633
- I like it.

618
00:25:30,800 --> 00:25:32,533
- [Bill] For God's
sake, open the door!

619
00:25:32,533 --> 00:25:34,500
- [Mike] Hey, it's
Johnny Tremaine.

620
00:25:34,500 --> 00:25:36,267
- Keep your head on! I'm coming!

621
00:25:37,600 --> 00:25:40,333
- [Tom] You won't
believe the day I've had.

622
00:25:40,333 --> 00:25:41,633
- A terrible accident.

623
00:25:41,633 --> 00:25:43,933
I've got to save her,
I've got to save her.

624
00:25:43,933 --> 00:25:45,333
- What is it? What
have you got there?

625
00:25:45,333 --> 00:25:46,800
- Kurt, please.
- It's mine.

626
00:25:46,800 --> 00:25:48,367
- We'll have to tune some
instruments, quickly.

627
00:25:48,367 --> 00:25:49,900
- What are you going to do?

628
00:25:49,900 --> 00:25:51,067
Aren't you going to have a
look in the closet first?

629
00:25:51,067 --> 00:25:52,733
- No, I can't now.
This is more important.

630
00:25:52,733 --> 00:25:53,933
- But you don't understand.

631
00:25:53,933 --> 00:25:55,100
- For God's sake,
Kurt! This is urgent.

632
00:25:55,100 --> 00:25:56,367
Do as I tell you
before it's too late.

633
00:25:56,367 --> 00:25:57,933
I can't waste precious
time arguing with you.

634
00:25:57,933 --> 00:26:00,367
- [Mike] How was my day?
Oh, it's not important.

635
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:05,067
- No time to mourn.
Got to get to work.

636
00:26:05,067 --> 00:26:07,900
(suspenseful music)

637
00:26:07,900 --> 00:26:09,633
- [Mike] Okay. And pencils down.

638
00:26:09,633 --> 00:26:11,133
How much did you risk? Ooh.

639
00:26:12,600 --> 00:26:13,900
- [Tom] This'll
get my pinot noir

640
00:26:13,900 --> 00:26:15,833
that Tawny knows I've
been looking for.

641
00:26:20,167 --> 00:26:21,867
- [Mike] So he's
a scientist, then.

642
00:26:21,867 --> 00:26:22,867
- [Tom] Pretty much.

643
00:26:25,600 --> 00:26:29,467
- Is it honey he's putting
in the KC Masterpiece sauce?

644
00:26:31,667 --> 00:26:34,467
- Dude, he's wiring
his lab for KWAN.

645
00:26:34,467 --> 00:26:35,800
- Pro Logic.
- Huh.

646
00:26:39,733 --> 00:26:42,033
- [Mike] Man. I am tearing
through the Brawny.

647
00:26:45,533 --> 00:26:46,433
- Should I?

648
00:26:52,300 --> 00:26:53,667
- [Tom] One, two, three.

649
00:26:53,667 --> 00:26:56,000
Aw, cripes, that
looks about right.

650
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,400
As long as I'm close.
This isn't rocket science.

651
00:27:01,767 --> 00:27:03,767
- [Mike] I wonder how
dad's doing in Denver.

652
00:27:03,767 --> 00:27:05,033
Hope his flight was okay.

653
00:27:08,500 --> 00:27:11,033
- [Tom] I bet he's going to
turn her into Mrs. Olson.

654
00:27:11,033 --> 00:27:13,533
(Tom humming)

655
00:27:15,767 --> 00:27:20,233
- That flask has an
hourglass figure. It's hot.

656
00:27:20,233 --> 00:27:21,067
Oh baby.

657
00:27:23,600 --> 00:27:24,867
- [Mike] Well, his
methods are unusual,

658
00:27:24,867 --> 00:27:26,267
but his coffee's great.

659
00:27:29,167 --> 00:27:31,667
- [Tom] Well, a little of this
couldn't hurt, what the hell.

660
00:27:33,100 --> 00:27:36,767
- Oh, you put the lime in the
coconut and drink it all up!

661
00:27:36,767 --> 00:27:39,667
- [Mike] Say,
Doctor. It's Darkman.

662
00:27:41,933 --> 00:27:44,467
- [Tom] Done. Now time
to make my own Bailey's.

663
00:27:45,933 --> 00:27:47,933
- With a Matthews clock, you
always know what time it is.

664
00:27:47,933 --> 00:27:49,033
Now back to our-

665
00:27:52,200 --> 00:27:54,100
- [Mike] This is the most
complicated beer bong

666
00:27:54,100 --> 00:27:55,167
I've ever seen.

667
00:27:56,700 --> 00:27:58,000
(Tom humming)

668
00:27:58,000 --> 00:27:59,733
"An American in Vitro".

669
00:28:03,933 --> 00:28:06,233
- They saved Sister
Bertrille's brain.

670
00:28:08,200 --> 00:28:10,700
- [Tom] Boss, you've broken
the goofy meter again.

671
00:28:11,967 --> 00:28:15,300
- I saw them move.

672
00:28:15,300 --> 00:28:16,233
It can't be.

673
00:28:16,233 --> 00:28:17,167
- [Tom] She won't be able to do

674
00:28:17,167 --> 00:28:18,933
any heavy lifting for a while.

675
00:28:18,933 --> 00:28:20,700
- My eyes are deceiving me.

676
00:28:22,100 --> 00:28:23,300
- What you see is real.

677
00:28:23,300 --> 00:28:24,867
- [Mike] What you
smell is unfortunate.

678
00:28:24,867 --> 00:28:27,100
- What's done is done and
what I've done is right.

679
00:28:28,333 --> 00:28:30,200
It's the work of science.

680
00:28:30,200 --> 00:28:32,033
- Science? I thought
you said pizza.

681
00:28:35,367 --> 00:28:38,000
(Jan whimpering)

682
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:40,067
- [Tom] Could you
scratch my nose, honey?

683
00:28:41,667 --> 00:28:43,167
(Jan whimpering)

684
00:28:43,167 --> 00:28:44,567
She talks like Clutch Cargo.

685
00:28:48,433 --> 00:28:50,267
- Doesn't she need lungs?

686
00:28:50,267 --> 00:28:51,633
[Tom] No, she's got neck juice.

687
00:28:51,633 --> 00:28:52,467
- Oh.

688
00:28:55,200 --> 00:28:56,300
- Let me die.

689
00:28:57,467 --> 00:28:58,967
- [Tom] I'm sorry?

690
00:29:00,233 --> 00:29:01,433
- Let me die.

691
00:29:01,433 --> 00:29:02,667
- [Mike] Oh, you're
welcome, honey.

692
00:29:04,100 --> 00:29:05,700
- I've had success
with transplants.

693
00:29:05,700 --> 00:29:07,067
Now I can do it for her.

694
00:29:07,067 --> 00:29:08,667
- You can change
your hair color,

695
00:29:08,667 --> 00:29:10,133
give her a complete makeover.

696
00:29:11,833 --> 00:29:13,800
- Transplant her to what?

697
00:29:13,800 --> 00:29:14,933
- Larry Storch.

698
00:29:14,933 --> 00:29:16,300
- I'll bring her back.

699
00:29:16,300 --> 00:29:19,033
She'll live and I'll
get her another body.

700
00:29:19,033 --> 00:29:20,667
I can make her complete again.

701
00:29:20,667 --> 00:29:22,733
- [Tom] A little shorter
maybe, but still.

702
00:29:22,733 --> 00:29:24,467
- Only a madman can believe

703
00:29:24,467 --> 00:29:26,500
that she could ever
be like before.

704
00:29:26,500 --> 00:29:27,600
- Don't argue with me.

705
00:29:28,567 --> 00:29:29,500
- Okay.
- I love her too much

706
00:29:29,500 --> 00:29:30,933
to let her stay like this.

707
00:29:31,967 --> 00:29:33,967
I'll restore her as before.

708
00:29:33,967 --> 00:29:34,800
You'll see.

709
00:29:36,300 --> 00:29:38,833
- Can't you realize?
Can't you see?

710
00:29:38,833 --> 00:29:41,300
There's a pattern
to all that lives.

711
00:29:41,300 --> 00:29:43,867
An order, an arrangement.

712
00:29:43,867 --> 00:29:46,467
She had a heart and a brain.

713
00:29:46,467 --> 00:29:47,967
- And the nerve!

714
00:29:47,967 --> 00:29:49,167
- Her spirit was in both,
not in one or the other.

715
00:29:49,167 --> 00:29:50,833
- [Mike] Now she's
a paperweight.

716
00:29:52,033 --> 00:29:53,667
- I'll give her a
brain and a heart.

717
00:29:53,667 --> 00:29:55,467
- Yes, and what of her soul?

718
00:29:55,467 --> 00:29:56,467
- It's in the car.

719
00:29:57,933 --> 00:29:59,667
- You say you love her,

720
00:29:59,667 --> 00:30:01,733
and you can remember
her love for you.

721
00:30:01,733 --> 00:30:03,933
- [Tom] As you can see folks,
no strings, no mirrors.

722
00:30:03,933 --> 00:30:05,833
- Yet you've made of her
an experiment of horror.

723
00:30:05,833 --> 00:30:08,133
- [Mike] Shut up, man.
You're lucky my chick's here.

724
00:30:08,133 --> 00:30:10,867
- All the skill in science I
possess was meant for this.

725
00:30:10,867 --> 00:30:12,467
Life has a pattern.

726
00:30:13,800 --> 00:30:15,600
The whole pattern of my
life is shaping itself

727
00:30:15,600 --> 00:30:16,533
to save her now.

728
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:19,533
- Then you intend to
go through with it?

729
00:30:19,533 --> 00:30:20,767
- Yes.

730
00:30:20,767 --> 00:30:21,933
- Well, at least you don't have

731
00:30:21,933 --> 00:30:24,467
delusions of
grandeur or anything.

732
00:30:24,467 --> 00:30:25,467
- Sleep, my darling.

733
00:30:27,300 --> 00:30:28,667
Rest and grow strong.

734
00:30:28,667 --> 00:30:30,500
- [Tom] Can I get
a neck pillow, hon?

735
00:30:33,433 --> 00:30:34,600
- [Mike] Okay, now quiet, Kurt.

736
00:30:34,600 --> 00:30:36,267
I just put her
down for the night.

737
00:30:39,033 --> 00:30:41,800
- Come here. I've got a great
idea for a practical joke.

738
00:30:42,867 --> 00:30:44,133
- [Mike] Put her neck in water.

739
00:30:44,133 --> 00:30:44,967
- How long do you think
we can keep her alive

740
00:30:44,967 --> 00:30:46,233
under these conditions?

741
00:30:46,233 --> 00:30:47,567
- [Mike] Til she gets
pan sores, I guess.

742
00:30:48,733 --> 00:30:50,100
- 50 Hours at the most.

743
00:30:52,333 --> 00:30:56,400
- Do you really believe you
can work a transplant on her?

744
00:30:56,400 --> 00:30:57,700
- [Tom] Nope.

745
00:30:57,700 --> 00:30:58,667
- Successfully?

746
00:30:58,667 --> 00:30:59,833
- Yes.
- Uh, no.

747
00:31:02,233 --> 00:31:03,400
- Like my arm.

748
00:31:04,533 --> 00:31:06,000
Withered and deformed.

749
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:07,467
- [Tom] His left
hand's pretty bad too.

750
00:31:07,467 --> 00:31:09,667
- Yours was an early
experiment that failed.

751
00:31:10,867 --> 00:31:12,733
With her, I'm using
my new adreno serum.

752
00:31:12,733 --> 00:31:13,567
- Oh, the neck juice.

753
00:31:13,567 --> 00:31:14,867
- It must work.

754
00:31:14,867 --> 00:31:17,033
- [Mike] It's got no
fat and half the salt.

755
00:31:18,367 --> 00:31:19,433
- I've got to go now.

756
00:31:20,433 --> 00:31:24,000
If the police or anyone call.

757
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:25,733
- [Tom] Have her get it.

758
00:31:25,733 --> 00:31:28,100
- Tell them you don't
know anything about it.

759
00:31:28,100 --> 00:31:29,567
- [Mike] Honey.

760
00:31:29,567 --> 00:31:30,767
- I don't think anyone
will trace us here because

761
00:31:30,767 --> 00:31:32,133
her body was burned
in the wreckage.

762
00:31:33,033 --> 00:31:34,467
- Yes, of course, of course.

763
00:31:34,467 --> 00:31:35,700
Look, Bill, before you go.

764
00:31:35,700 --> 00:31:37,167
- Could you give
me a sponge bath?

765
00:31:37,167 --> 00:31:38,400
- Could you have a
look in that closet?

766
00:31:38,400 --> 00:31:39,867
It's the reason why
I called you up here.

767
00:31:39,867 --> 00:31:42,367
- [Tom] Oh, so that's why you
had me kill my girlfriend.

768
00:31:42,367 --> 00:31:44,100
So I could see in the closet?

769
00:31:44,100 --> 00:31:45,500
- [Crow] It looks
really nice in there.

770
00:31:45,500 --> 00:31:47,300
I got a new organizer
and everything.

771
00:31:50,467 --> 00:31:52,200
- [Mike] God speed, my friend.

772
00:31:53,633 --> 00:31:55,467
- Okay, here he comes,
here he comes. Shh.

773
00:31:55,467 --> 00:31:58,833
You need to keep it
down. Okay, shh, shh.

774
00:32:01,167 --> 00:32:03,700
- [Tom] You knuckle-knob.
What do you have in here?

775
00:32:05,733 --> 00:32:07,767
- Sounds like crazy
Guggenheims in there.

776
00:32:11,833 --> 00:32:13,433
- [Tom] Hello, hello?

777
00:32:13,433 --> 00:32:15,333
Looks like the Soupy Sales show.

778
00:32:16,567 --> 00:32:17,667
- Hello, hello, hello, hello.

779
00:32:19,900 --> 00:32:23,467
(creature growling)

780
00:32:23,467 --> 00:32:26,067
- [Mike] Ew, Dylan Thomas'
last moments on Earth.

781
00:32:30,967 --> 00:32:31,900
- Keep it locked.

782
00:32:31,900 --> 00:32:33,767
- And keep the litter box clean.

783
00:32:33,767 --> 00:32:35,267
- It almost broke out.

784
00:32:37,033 --> 00:32:38,667
- Don't worry about it.
Just keep it closed.

785
00:32:38,667 --> 00:32:39,867
- [Tom] I thought you'd love it.

786
00:32:39,867 --> 00:32:41,200
Didn't just see the
hat I put on it?

787
00:32:43,600 --> 00:32:44,867
- [Mike] Well, thank
you for showing me that.

788
00:32:44,867 --> 00:32:47,267
It made my day a
little bit brighter.

789
00:32:50,567 --> 00:32:52,133
- I have to find her a body.

790
00:32:52,133 --> 00:32:54,200
- How are you going to
go about getting one?

791
00:32:56,200 --> 00:32:58,900
- I'm not even gonna
dignify that with an answer.

792
00:32:58,900 --> 00:33:00,267
- Bill, how will you do it?

793
00:33:01,700 --> 00:33:03,267
- There are ways.

794
00:33:03,267 --> 00:33:06,367
(Tom laughing maniacally)

795
00:33:06,367 --> 00:33:07,367
There are ways.

796
00:33:07,367 --> 00:33:09,867
- [Mike] Lots of
ways, many ways.

797
00:33:09,867 --> 00:33:11,233
Have I mentioned there are ways?

798
00:33:13,233 --> 00:33:14,100
- [Tom] Ways.

799
00:33:15,867 --> 00:33:18,433
(upbeat music)

800
00:33:46,933 --> 00:33:49,200
- [Mike] Hey, he walked
all the way to Paris.

801
00:33:50,567 --> 00:33:52,667
(sensual music)

802
00:33:52,667 --> 00:33:55,533
- [Mike] Say, this may
actually be an upgrade.

803
00:33:58,167 --> 00:33:59,767
- [Tom] She can't
have a cardboard body.

804
00:33:59,767 --> 00:34:00,833
I've ruled that out.

805
00:34:02,267 --> 00:34:05,600
- [Mike] My name is Dirk,
what's yours? (coughing)

806
00:34:09,867 --> 00:34:14,200
- Ooh, this is the one big
silver lining of my day.

807
00:34:14,200 --> 00:34:16,933
- [Tom] It's nice and all,
but I want something sleazy.

808
00:34:21,933 --> 00:34:24,100
- [Mike] If Jack
Ruby owned a Denny's.

809
00:34:26,200 --> 00:34:27,933
- I hope that's not Rose Marie.

810
00:34:30,200 --> 00:34:31,833
- [Mike] Hey look, the
panel from "What's My Line?"

811
00:34:31,833 --> 00:34:32,933
is behind her.

812
00:34:34,900 --> 00:34:37,667
- [Tom] Pasties and a
g-string, beer and a sharp,

813
00:34:37,667 --> 00:34:40,733
boil 'em through a shot glass
and buffalo squeeze, ha ha.

814
00:34:45,233 --> 00:34:46,667
- He's helping me. I'm fine.

815
00:34:48,100 --> 00:34:50,800
- [Tom] Our soup of the day
is cauliflower au gratin.

816
00:34:50,800 --> 00:34:54,300
Today's special is Salisbury
steak and German potato salad.

817
00:34:54,300 --> 00:34:56,633
- [Mike] Oh, well, I'll
just see a menu, thank you.

818
00:34:56,633 --> 00:34:58,267
- I am the menu.

819
00:35:02,000 --> 00:35:05,067
- [Tom] I'm just curious.
What size collar do you wear?

820
00:35:10,767 --> 00:35:13,367
- Senor Wences and
Evita, out on the town.

821
00:35:14,600 --> 00:35:15,533
Talk to your hand.

822
00:35:16,867 --> 00:35:18,867
- [Tom] Welcome to
the Diane Arbus Cafe.

823
00:35:21,900 --> 00:35:24,700
- A night on the town
in Escanaba, Michigan.

824
00:35:26,333 --> 00:35:28,300
- [Mike] This dance
represents that there

825
00:35:28,300 --> 00:35:31,167
are no baked potatoes
til after five.

826
00:35:33,567 --> 00:35:35,733
- [Tom] Jojo was a man who
thought he was a woman.

827
00:35:37,567 --> 00:35:40,133
(Tom clucking)

828
00:35:44,233 --> 00:35:47,500
- The spokesmodel competition.

829
00:35:47,500 --> 00:35:48,800
- Rocky Graziano!

830
00:35:51,900 --> 00:35:54,500
- [Mike] I must have blood
before the night is done.

831
00:35:58,733 --> 00:36:00,500
- [Tom] Hey gang, there's
a snuff film playing

832
00:36:00,500 --> 00:36:01,900
over at the Rialto, let's go!

833
00:36:01,900 --> 00:36:04,500
(all cheering)

834
00:36:06,167 --> 00:36:09,833
- Oh, wait for me.
(laughing awkwardly)

835
00:36:12,900 --> 00:36:15,333
- [Mike] Mr. Graziano,
can I have your autograph?

836
00:36:17,767 --> 00:36:22,033
♪ I'm getting
sentimental over you ♪

837
00:36:23,100 --> 00:36:24,333
- Boy, what a shift.

838
00:36:28,500 --> 00:36:29,767
- [Mike] He's rocking.

839
00:36:29,767 --> 00:36:30,967
Who would've thought
a few hours earlier

840
00:36:30,967 --> 00:36:33,000
he was cradling his
wife's head in his lap.

841
00:36:34,767 --> 00:36:37,067
- [Tom] The 1958 floozy.

842
00:36:38,900 --> 00:36:41,667
(Crow squeaking)

843
00:36:43,933 --> 00:36:46,767
- Oh, hi. Have you
seen my chests?

844
00:36:48,267 --> 00:36:51,567
So, you a goer, eh? Goer,
nudge nudge, know what I mean?

845
00:36:58,167 --> 00:37:00,433
- Meanwhile, at
Kurt Cobain's house.

846
00:37:00,433 --> 00:37:01,900
Oh.

847
00:37:01,900 --> 00:37:04,033
- [Mike] Dave is such a
dink for not letting me die.

848
00:37:08,533 --> 00:37:10,633
- He's keeping her
alive with Grey Poupon.

849
00:37:14,867 --> 00:37:17,933
- [Tom] Boy, that was
an important scene, huh?

850
00:37:17,933 --> 00:37:19,067
- [Mike] So you're
you're saying you're

851
00:37:19,067 --> 00:37:20,367
not into dismemberment?

852
00:37:26,300 --> 00:37:29,200
- Well, back into
the broom closet.

853
00:37:33,100 --> 00:37:34,633
- Here's Abe Burrows
and Jimmy Hoffa

854
00:37:34,633 --> 00:37:36,200
entertaining Eleanor Roosevelt.

855
00:37:40,800 --> 00:37:42,467
- [Mike] Don't
wait up, everyone.

856
00:37:46,567 --> 00:37:49,400
- [Tom] Ma'am, may I pour you
a buttock, a nip, a drink?

857
00:37:50,600 --> 00:37:52,667
- What took you so long?

858
00:37:52,667 --> 00:37:54,467
- What made you think I'd come?

859
00:37:54,467 --> 00:37:56,933
- You know a good
thing when you see it.

860
00:37:56,933 --> 00:37:58,100
- Yeah, there was plenty to see.

861
00:37:58,100 --> 00:37:59,333
- And the Skillet
Scrambler was numtious.

862
00:37:59,333 --> 00:38:00,900
- I liked your act.

863
00:38:00,900 --> 00:38:02,367
- Is that all you liked?

864
00:38:02,367 --> 00:38:05,867
- Well, your costume, what there
was of it, was interesting.

865
00:38:05,867 --> 00:38:07,333
- Is that all?

866
00:38:07,333 --> 00:38:08,533
- [Tom] Well, I liked
your Nicholson impression.

867
00:38:08,533 --> 00:38:11,433
- The rest of the
equipment is standard.

868
00:38:11,433 --> 00:38:14,667
But, uh, the arrangement
is pretty special.

869
00:38:14,667 --> 00:38:17,167
- [Mike] Oh, by Elia Kazan.

870
00:38:17,167 --> 00:38:20,033
- You're no tourist just
looking at the sights.

871
00:38:20,033 --> 00:38:22,500
What are you doing
here? Slumming?

872
00:38:22,500 --> 00:38:24,333
- Well, I get my
allowance once a week.

873
00:38:24,333 --> 00:38:27,800
- Okay, if you're so loaded,
you pay for the drink.

874
00:38:27,800 --> 00:38:28,800
- I already did.

875
00:38:28,800 --> 00:38:30,000
- Oh, I ate your
shift meal, too.

876
00:38:30,000 --> 00:38:31,633
- Are you hustling
for the house?

877
00:38:31,633 --> 00:38:32,967
- I hustle for myself.

878
00:38:32,967 --> 00:38:34,233
- [Mike] Oh, sorry
I offended you.

879
00:38:34,233 --> 00:38:35,867
You really have a
lot of integrity.

880
00:38:37,067 --> 00:38:39,200
- I'm the leading
lady around here.

881
00:38:39,200 --> 00:38:41,900
I can dance for the
squares out front,

882
00:38:41,900 --> 00:38:44,933
or I can relax back
here with my friends.

883
00:38:46,333 --> 00:38:48,367
- I'll bet you don't have
an enemy in the world.

884
00:38:48,367 --> 00:38:51,433
- [Tom] Well, I had a
falling out with Delta Burke.

885
00:38:51,433 --> 00:38:52,867
Who hasn't?

886
00:38:52,867 --> 00:38:54,600
- Hi, I'm Casey Kasem.

887
00:38:54,600 --> 00:38:58,000
This one goes out to a heartsick
lover with a severed head.

888
00:38:59,200 --> 00:39:00,733
- [Mike] Finally,
I'm a size eight.

889
00:39:04,900 --> 00:39:06,100
- Let me die.

890
00:39:06,100 --> 00:39:07,600
- [Tom] Are you
still on that kick?

891
00:39:07,600 --> 00:39:10,067
Come on, pull yourself up by
the collar tabs and get a job.

892
00:39:13,967 --> 00:39:15,067
- Let me die.

893
00:39:16,200 --> 00:39:17,667
- After you've
cleaned your room.

894
00:39:19,033 --> 00:39:21,600
- [Mike] To recap, let me die.

895
00:39:21,600 --> 00:39:23,333
- Where are you from?

896
00:39:23,333 --> 00:39:24,400
- Oh, around.

897
00:39:24,400 --> 00:39:26,267
Just looking around.

898
00:39:26,267 --> 00:39:29,500
- When you get done
looking, then what?

899
00:39:30,433 --> 00:39:31,667
- I operate.

900
00:39:31,667 --> 00:39:34,567
- I get your message.
It's coming across.

901
00:39:34,567 --> 00:39:36,267
- [Tom] So, uh, what do you do?

902
00:39:36,267 --> 00:39:39,400
- You could flip any chick
in the house. Why me?

903
00:39:39,400 --> 00:39:40,600
- [Mike] Why not scramble me?

904
00:39:40,600 --> 00:39:42,467
- Well, like I
said, I'm looking.

905
00:39:42,467 --> 00:39:45,200
- And you don't have
to look any further.

906
00:39:45,200 --> 00:39:46,833
I'm not gonna fake it for you.

907
00:39:48,000 --> 00:39:50,433
- When do you go on
again, huh? When?

908
00:39:50,433 --> 00:39:51,800
- Relax.

909
00:39:51,800 --> 00:39:54,567
I got another show yet,
but it could be for you.

910
00:39:55,567 --> 00:39:56,633
- I was just asking.

911
00:39:57,967 --> 00:39:59,200
- I don't feel good.

912
00:39:59,200 --> 00:40:03,600
- You're shaking. Are
you getting nervous?

913
00:40:03,600 --> 00:40:05,133
I'm over 21.

914
00:40:05,133 --> 00:40:06,700
- [Tom] 21? She's over 41.

915
00:40:06,700 --> 00:40:08,000
- Oh, it isn't that, it's...

916
00:40:08,000 --> 00:40:09,200
I just got things to do.

917
00:40:09,200 --> 00:40:11,367
Things to take care of.

918
00:40:12,533 --> 00:40:13,933
- Well, if doing that
will make you sick,

919
00:40:13,933 --> 00:40:15,167
what could be more important

920
00:40:15,167 --> 00:40:17,433
than to be taken
care of right now?

921
00:40:17,433 --> 00:40:18,767
- Are you coming on to me?

922
00:40:20,167 --> 00:40:23,367
- But I've, I've got to
see about helping somebody,

923
00:40:25,033 --> 00:40:26,367
somebody who needs
my help very badly.

924
00:40:26,367 --> 00:40:28,300
- [Mike] Hey, this,
this is sick. Ugh.

925
00:40:29,467 --> 00:40:31,267
- Not when you've got
me feeling so good.

926
00:40:32,300 --> 00:40:33,900
I'm so warm all over.

927
00:40:35,300 --> 00:40:39,333
I know I'm good for you, I know
I'm good for what you want.

928
00:40:39,333 --> 00:40:40,967
- [Tom] They are talking
about sex, aren't they?

929
00:40:40,967 --> 00:40:42,500
- Really?
- I thought so.

930
00:40:42,500 --> 00:40:44,267
- You may be just
what I'm looking for.

931
00:40:46,200 --> 00:40:48,567
- [Mike] Ew.
(Crow whimpering)

932
00:40:48,567 --> 00:40:51,100
- Lucy Arnaz is back
and she's pissed.

933
00:40:51,100 --> 00:40:53,033
- You've got your nerve.

934
00:40:53,033 --> 00:40:55,067
- Oh, look who's talking.

935
00:40:56,267 --> 00:40:57,533
- Why don't you haul
your beat-up body

936
00:40:57,533 --> 00:40:59,767
back to the bar with
the rest of the flies?

937
00:40:59,767 --> 00:41:01,333
- Keep your g-string on.

938
00:41:01,333 --> 00:41:03,933
I only came in here
to change my clothes.

939
00:41:03,933 --> 00:41:05,567
(all shouting)

940
00:41:05,567 --> 00:41:08,200
I'd like to see the
rest of the show.

941
00:41:08,200 --> 00:41:11,200
- Come back in half an
hour and maybe you will.

942
00:41:11,200 --> 00:41:12,433
- Get lost.

943
00:41:12,433 --> 00:41:13,700
- [Tom] Could you
get rid of your dad?

944
00:41:13,700 --> 00:41:15,533
- Hi, lover boy.

945
00:41:15,533 --> 00:41:16,367
- Same to you.

946
00:41:19,233 --> 00:41:22,900
- Hey, come here. Don't hide.

947
00:41:22,900 --> 00:41:24,733
You know, you've
got the kind of face

948
00:41:24,733 --> 00:41:26,500
a girl doesn't mind looking at.

949
00:41:26,500 --> 00:41:29,367
Even up front, all the other
girls were asking about you.

950
00:41:29,367 --> 00:41:30,867
- [Mike] Really?
What'd they say?

951
00:41:30,867 --> 00:41:33,333
- Get out of here. Two's
company, three's a crowd.

952
00:41:33,333 --> 00:41:34,567
- [Tom] Zing.

953
00:41:34,567 --> 00:41:36,467
- Who's to tell me to
blow if I don't want to?

954
00:41:36,467 --> 00:41:40,267
This here's my dressing
room too, remember.

955
00:41:40,267 --> 00:41:42,567
- It kills her to
see me make time.

956
00:41:42,567 --> 00:41:44,733
- You're the only thing
that's going to be made

957
00:41:44,733 --> 00:41:46,767
around here tonight, honey.

958
00:41:46,767 --> 00:41:48,533
- Eat your heart out.

959
00:41:48,533 --> 00:41:49,500
- [Tom] Well.

960
00:41:49,500 --> 00:41:51,600
- Well, I'd better be going.

961
00:41:51,600 --> 00:41:53,367
- It's been sleazy
and all, but, uh.

962
00:41:53,367 --> 00:41:55,200
- What for?
- Look what you've done.

963
00:41:55,200 --> 00:41:56,267
- Oh, no, no.

964
00:41:57,200 --> 00:41:58,433
- No no no no no, stop.

965
00:41:58,433 --> 00:42:00,700
- Hey, desist, don't you!

966
00:42:00,700 --> 00:42:02,433
Don't you.
- Freeze.

967
00:42:02,433 --> 00:42:03,700
- Oh no!

968
00:42:03,700 --> 00:42:05,767
(all shouting)

969
00:42:05,767 --> 00:42:07,000
- I'll remember you.

970
00:42:08,100 --> 00:42:09,633
- Yeah, that's
what I'm afraid of.

971
00:42:14,433 --> 00:42:16,267
- [Mike] Do you
think he likes me?

972
00:42:16,267 --> 00:42:17,700
- You lousy tramp.

973
00:42:17,700 --> 00:42:20,367
Once in a blue moon I latch
onto a man with class,

974
00:42:20,367 --> 00:42:22,467
and you messed it up.

975
00:42:22,467 --> 00:42:23,767
- [Mike] What?

976
00:42:23,767 --> 00:42:26,400
- What makes him
think you had him?

977
00:42:26,400 --> 00:42:28,067
He wouldn't have you on a bet.

978
00:42:28,067 --> 00:42:29,667
- Says who?

979
00:42:29,667 --> 00:42:30,967
- Says me.

980
00:42:30,967 --> 00:42:33,900
What's a guy like that
want leftovers for?

981
00:42:33,900 --> 00:42:34,967
- Leftovers?

982
00:42:36,000 --> 00:42:37,100
- Shannon Doherty, no!

983
00:42:38,533 --> 00:42:41,200
- [Tom] Yes. Danny Bonaduce
in the fight of his life.

984
00:42:42,367 --> 00:42:43,733
This is grotesque. I
gotta get out of here.

985
00:42:43,733 --> 00:42:45,000
Take us out of this.

986
00:42:45,000 --> 00:42:46,667
- No, finally something
I can really get behind.

987
00:42:46,667 --> 00:42:50,067
Why don't you guys,
okay, I'm outta here.

988
00:42:53,633 --> 00:42:55,600
(cat meowing)

989
00:42:55,600 --> 00:42:58,133
(upbeat music)

990
00:43:26,333 --> 00:43:27,500
- Well, new feller.

991
00:43:27,500 --> 00:43:28,733
Gotta hand it to you.

992
00:43:28,733 --> 00:43:31,000
You got us going on
a project right away,

993
00:43:31,000 --> 00:43:34,267
having us design hats for the
lady in the movie, why you.

994
00:43:35,200 --> 00:43:36,700
- I just feel so sorry for her.

995
00:43:36,700 --> 00:43:39,033
Everything she must
be going through.

996
00:43:39,033 --> 00:43:42,867
Her fiance is mental, and
she's lost her independence.

997
00:43:42,867 --> 00:43:45,600
- Not to mention trying to
minimize your major figure flaw.

998
00:43:45,600 --> 00:43:47,033
- Yeah.

999
00:43:47,033 --> 00:43:48,300
- Well, I've always
thought that the right hat

1000
00:43:48,300 --> 00:43:49,800
can make you look
and feel terrific.

1001
00:43:49,800 --> 00:43:52,767
And that's why for her, I
designed a bowling bag hat.

1002
00:43:52,767 --> 00:43:54,167
She can zip it around herself

1003
00:43:54,167 --> 00:43:55,700
and just be toted anywhere.

1004
00:43:55,700 --> 00:43:57,000
- That's really clever there.

1005
00:43:57,000 --> 00:43:59,600
You know, my idea
was simply a crown

1006
00:43:59,600 --> 00:44:02,567
for that regal Katharine
Hepburn "Lion in Winter" look.

1007
00:44:04,100 --> 00:44:05,600
- Personally, I can't wear hats,

1008
00:44:05,600 --> 00:44:07,700
but I thought she'd looked
neat in my chianti hat.

1009
00:44:07,700 --> 00:44:09,800
- Oh, that is neat. Yeah.

1010
00:44:09,800 --> 00:44:11,100
- Very nice, very nice.

1011
00:44:11,100 --> 00:44:12,567
- You know, as a fellow woman,

1012
00:44:12,567 --> 00:44:15,300
I knew she'd want to create
the illusion of height.

1013
00:44:15,300 --> 00:44:18,533
So I came up with
this crown roast hat.

1014
00:44:18,533 --> 00:44:22,033
Which also serves six.

1015
00:44:22,033 --> 00:44:25,867
With a side of snow
peas and new potatoes.

1016
00:44:25,867 --> 00:44:27,233
- Well, I hope
you like this one.

1017
00:44:27,233 --> 00:44:28,500
I worked really hard on it.

1018
00:44:28,500 --> 00:44:30,400
To me, it sounded really cool

1019
00:44:30,400 --> 00:44:32,167
to give her a kinetic ball hat.

1020
00:44:33,200 --> 00:44:34,667
A real conversation starter.

1021
00:44:34,667 --> 00:44:37,800
- Put it on the
desk, aw. It works.

1022
00:44:37,800 --> 00:44:41,167
- This raspberry beret
just screamed fun to me.

1023
00:44:41,167 --> 00:44:43,833
- Well, let me just say
that form follows function

1024
00:44:43,833 --> 00:44:45,900
in my lazy Susan hat.

1025
00:44:45,900 --> 00:44:48,767
This way, she's really
the center of attention

1026
00:44:48,767 --> 00:44:50,367
at any dinner function.

1027
00:44:50,367 --> 00:44:52,933
(all laughing)

1028
00:44:56,100 --> 00:44:58,100
- Well? Fun, huh?

1029
00:44:58,100 --> 00:44:59,567
- Yeah, fun.

1030
00:44:59,567 --> 00:45:01,100
- I don't know.

1031
00:45:01,100 --> 00:45:03,367
I just have this feeling
she's really a shoes person.

1032
00:45:04,867 --> 00:45:05,700
- Huh.

1033
00:45:05,700 --> 00:45:07,800
(alarm blaring)

1034
00:45:07,800 --> 00:45:10,400
(all shouting)

1035
00:45:21,100 --> 00:45:22,733
- [Mike] Well, that
was my project, anyway.

1036
00:45:22,733 --> 00:45:24,667
- No, it was very good.
- Thank you.

1037
00:45:24,667 --> 00:45:25,733
- It was fun.

1038
00:45:26,967 --> 00:45:28,467
- [Mike] Oh, yeah.

1039
00:45:28,467 --> 00:45:30,067
- [Jan] What has he done to you?

1040
00:45:30,067 --> 00:45:32,900
- [Tom] What hasn't
he done to me?

1041
00:45:32,900 --> 00:45:35,400
